  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 410 PM EDT WED JUL 22 2026\n\nThe long term period will be characterized by upper level ridging \nover the western CONUS and an upper level trough over the \nnortheastern CONUS, with anomalously high 500 mb heights over much \nof the central and western CONUS. Locally, eastern Kentucky will \nfind itself in a region of northwest flow aloft for nearly the \nentire duration of the long term as the Commonwealth becomes \nsandwiched between these upper level features. This will allow for \nmultiple shortwaves to rotate through the forecast area, bringing \nadditional chances for rain throughout the forecast period.\n\nOn Friday, an approaching shortwave will allow for modest height \nfalls across the Ohio Valley, with surface troughing in the vicinity \nof the KY/VA line. Concurrently, surface winds will shift to be more \nnorthwesterly to westerly, ushering in a period of moisture \nadvection. By Friday afternoon, dewpoints will rise into the low to \nmid 60s in the northwestern half of the forecast area, and into the \nupper 60s to perhaps low 70s in the southeastern portion of the \nforecast area. With northwesterly flow through much of the column, \nprecipitable water will still remain below average in the northern \nportion of our forecast area; however, moisture quality will be \nbetter farther south, which will allow for some isolated showers and \nperhaps a rumble of thunder close to the VA state line. Elsewhere, \nweather is expected to remain dry, although rain chances will \nincrease somewhat in the southern portion of the forecast area \novernight as an additional shortwave approaches, with the best \nchance for rain still near the KY/TN line. Rain chances remain \nlowest in our northern counties, where the air mass will be \nmarginally drier. Here, skies may remain clear enough to allow for \njust enough radiative cooling to set up a mild ridge/valley \ntemperature split; however, this remains dependent on the magnitude \nof cloud cover overnight. Rain chances remain in the forecast \nthrough the weekend, primarily for the southern half of the area as \nadditional impulses traverse the Commonwealth; however, uncertainty \nin the timing of these features still remains high.\n\nWe begin to see more ridge riding impulses that move in from the \nnorthwest, as we get stuck in between the strengthening ridging to \nthe west and troughing in the east as mentioned above. This leaves \nthe Ohio Valley in a locally semi-zonal northwest flow pattern, as \nwe move into the first part of the work week. There does remain some \ntiming uncertainty with these impulses and this leads to more \nuncertainty in the forecast through the middle of next week. There \nare some decent signals showing up in the deterministic and ensemble \ndata that would suggest the potential for stronger storms, with \nsignals for increasing effective shear and CAPE profiles. This is \nshowing up well in the ECMWF extreme forecast index with increasing \nsignal for higher values climatologically for the combination of \nCAPE/shear. The signals are the strongest in the machine learning \nthe AI products as we get into the Monday and Tuesday timeframe. SPC \ndoes mention some signal showing up in the Ohio Valley, but still \nenough uncertainty to forgo any highlighted areas in the days 4-8 \noutlook period. This will have to be watched closely for future \noutlooks and updates in the NWP.",
